Attribute accumulation preserves ordering, duplicate handling, and diagnostic
precedence before external operations are validated.

  $ . ./setup.sh

Both orders of `mel.new` and `mel.send` construct a member. Repeated `mel.new`
remains idempotent, including after the combined operation has been selected.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > type t
  > external new_send : t -> string -> t = "Ctor" [@@mel.new] [@@mel.send]
  > external send_new : t -> string -> t = "Ctor" [@@mel.send] [@@mel.new]
  > external repeated_new : t -> string -> t = "Ctor"
  > [@@mel.new] [@@mel.new] [@@mel.send] [@@mel.new]
  > let make obj = (new_send obj "a", send_new obj "b", repeated_new obj "c")
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  // Generated by Melange
  'use strict';
  
  
  function make(obj) {
    return [
      new (obj.Ctor)("a"),
      new (obj.Ctor)("b"),
      new (obj.Ctor)("c")
    ];
  }
  
  module.exports = {
    make,
  }
  /* No side effect */

Unlike `mel.new`, a repeated `mel.send` is an error, also after `mel.new`.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > type t
  > external make : t -> t = "Ctor"
  > [@@mel.send] [@@mel.new] [@@mel.send]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", line 3, characters 25-37:
  3 | [@@mel.send] [@@mel.new] [@@mel.send]
                               ^^^^^^^^^^^^
  Error: Conflicting FFI attributes: `[@mel.send]' and `[@mel.send]' can't be
         specified at the same time
  [2]

A getter cannot import from a module.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > external get : int -> int = "value" [@@mel.get] [@@mel.module "package"]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", line 1, characters 0-72:
  1 | external get : int -> int = "value" [@@mel.get] [@@mel.module "package"]
      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  Error: Conflicting FFI attributes: Found an attribute that can't be used with
         `[@mel.get]'
  [2]

Argument validation runs before incompatible external-modifier diagnostics.
The invalid variadic argument takes precedence over the getter conflict.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > external get : int -> int = "value" [@@mel.get] [@@mel.variadic]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", line 1, characters 0-64:
  1 | external get : int -> int = "value" [@@mel.get] [@@mel.variadic]
      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  Error: `[@mel.variadic]' expects its last argument to be an array
  [2]

Once the argument is a valid variadic array, the getter conflict is reported.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > external get : int array -> int = "value" [@@mel.get] [@@mel.variadic]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", line 1, characters 0-70:
  1 | external get : int array -> int = "value" [@@mel.get] [@@mel.variadic]
      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  Error: Conflicting FFI attributes: Found an attribute that can't be used with
         `[@mel.get]'
  [2]

The same precedence applies to a send with a module binding.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > external send : int -> int = "run"
  > [@@mel.send] [@@mel.module "package"] [@@mel.variadic]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", lines 1-2, characters 0-54:
  1 | external send : int -> int = "run"
  2 | [@@mel.send] [@@mel.module "package"] [@@mel.variadic]
  Error: `[@mel.variadic]' expects its last argument to be an array
  [2]

And to a whole-module import with a scope.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > external call : int -> int = "package"
  > [@@mel.module] [@@mel.scope "nested"] [@@mel.variadic]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", lines 1-2, characters 0-54:
  1 | external call : int -> int = "package"
  2 | [@@mel.module] [@@mel.scope "nested"] [@@mel.variadic]
  Error: `[@mel.variadic]' expects its last argument to be an array
  [2]

Object creation validates conflicting modifiers before its arguments.

  $ cat > x.ml <<EOF
  > external make : int -> int = "" [@@mel.obj] [@@mel.module "package"]
  > EOF
  $ melc -ppx melppx -alert -unprocessed x.ml
  File "x.ml", line 1, characters 0-68:
  1 | external make : int -> int = "" [@@mel.obj] [@@mel.module "package"]
      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  Error: Found an attribute that conflicts with `[@mel.obj]'
  [2]
